.Dd September 25, 2026
.Dt NEXTBSD-FETCH 1
.Os NextBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm nextbsd-fetch
.Nd print the login banner
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl f
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints a cube and a few lines about the machine, the way a fetch tool does.
.Xr zsh 1
runs it from
.Pa /etc/zprofile
at login, and anyone can run it by hand.
.Pp
It reports the version from
.Pa /etc/os-release ,
the login shell, memory in use against the total, how long the machine has
been up, and the root filesystem.
.Pp
The last of those reads
.Li live media
when the root filesystem is a
.Xr unionfs 5 ,
which is how the live image is put together, and names the device otherwise.
.Pp
On live media a task list follows the banner: set a password for admin, set
the time zone, connect to wireless, and install NextBSD to disk, each with the
command that does it.
It answers to the same check as the
.Li storage
line, so the two cannot disagree, and an installed machine gets the banner
alone.
Only
.Xr passwd 1
is listed without
.Xr sudo 8 ;
it is setuid and changes your own account, where the other three write
.Pa /etc ,
bring up an interface, or partition a disk.
.Ss When it stays quiet
.Nm
prints nothing, and exits 0, in three cases.
.Bl -bullet
.It
Gershwin's login window is installed.
Something else asks who you are there, and a console that has already greeted
you answered first.
.It
.Pa ~/.hushlogin
exists.
That file is how a person asks for a quiet login, and
.Xr login 1
already honours it for the message of the day.
.It
Standard output is not a terminal.
.El
.Pp
.Fl f
overrides the first two, which is how to see the banner on a machine that has
a desktop.
.Ss Colour
Full colour needs a terminal that sets
.Ev COLORTERM
to
.Li truecolor
or
.Li 24bit .
Everything else with a usable
.Ev TERM
gets sixteen colours, where the cube's three faces collapse to three greys \(em
which is all the
.Xr vt 4
console can show in any case.
A terminal reporting
.Li dumb ,
and any non-terminal, gets no escape sequences at all.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl f
Print even where a login window is installed or
.Pa ~/.hushlogin
exists.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/System/Library/LaunchDaemons" -compact
.It Pa /etc/os-release
the version, from
.Li PRETTY_NAME
.It Pa ~/.hushlogin
silences this and the message of the day alike
.It Pa /etc/zprofile
runs this at login
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
See it on a machine with a desktop installed:
.Pp
.Dl $ nextbsd-fetch -f
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr login 1 ,
.Xr passwd 1 ,
.Xr zsh 1 ,
.Xr motd 5 ,
.Xr sudo 8 ,
.Xr tzsetup 8 ,
.Xr wlan 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Joseph Maloney
.Sh CAVEATS
The memory line reports a total alone where the free-page count cannot be
read, rather than a figure that would look like a full machine.
.Pp
The shell's version is shown only when
.Ev ZSH_VERSION
is in the environment, which
.Pa /etc/zprofile
arranges.
Run by hand elsewhere, the shell's name is printed without one rather than a
guess.
